menu "Prusa Remote Display validation"

    config PRD_VALIDATION_SCENARIOS
        bool "Compile the fixture-driven scenario flow"
        default n
        help
            Compiles the Milestone 2 Phase B bring-up flow: the deterministic fixture
            catalogue and scenario sequences, driving the real application store
            through real typed events and logging the resolved presentation after
            every accepted mutation.

            It is the on-device counterpart of the host reducer and projection
            suites: the same events, the same reducer, the same projection, running
            on the target. It renders nothing — no LVGL widget, no product scene —
            and it opens no socket.

            The BOOT button controls it while it runs: short press advances one step,
            long press pauses and resumes, very long press restarts the scenario.
            Those gestures are also dispatched into the store as the product facts
            they are, so what the model sees is unchanged by the bench controls.

            This is diagnostics, not product behaviour. Enable only in the
            hardware-validation profile; the debug and release profiles must leave it
            off so no fixture data reaches a product image.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_OPERATIONAL_WALK
        bool "Hold one operational state at a time, advanced by the button"
        default n
        help
            Walks the six operational fixtures — Ready, Preparing, Printing, Paused,
            Completed and Stopped — one at a time, holding each indefinitely until the BOOT
            button advances to the next.

            WHY THIS EXISTS. The scenario sweep moves on its own schedule, which makes it the
            wrong instrument for judging a composition: a state is on screen for a couple of
            seconds and the reviewer is racing a timer rather than looking. This holds a single
            state until someone decides they have seen enough of it.

            Each fixture is a list of real events folded through the real reducer, so what is
            held is a presentation the product can actually reach — not a hand-assembled state
            that might describe something impossible.

            Short press advances, long press goes back, very long press restarts at Ready. The
            gestures are also dispatched into the store as the product facts they are, exactly
            as the scenario flow does, so what the model sees is unchanged by the bench
            control.

            MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH PRD_VALIDATION_SCENARIOS: both drive the same store, and
            two drivers would interleave events neither could be held responsible for.

            This is diagnostics. Enable only in the hardware-validation profile.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_BRIGHTNESS_HARNESS
        bool "Walk the brightness levels, advanced only by the button"
        default n
        depends on PRD_UI_DIAGNOSTIC_SCENE
        help
            Holds one brightness level at a time, indefinitely, until a short BOOT press
            advances exactly one step. The PANEL carries a large step identifier, the full
            step label and a fixed neutral grey wedge; chat is not part of the test.

            THE SEQUENCE. C1-C4 walk the candidate On ceilings (50, 65, 80, 100 %). D1-D4
            walk the candidate Dimmed fractions of the reference ceiling's emitted light
            (30, 20, 12, 6 %). F1-F4 walk the production recession: 80 %, a two-second
            fade to 15 % of light, a labelled warning that the next press will darken the
            panel, the two-second fade to true off, and the wake back to 80 %. The next
            press returns to C1.

            WHY IT EXISTS, AND WHAT IT REPLACES. The 50 % ceiling and the Dimmed fraction
            are working baselines in no accepted Project Source, and both must be settled
            against the physical panel. An earlier instrument advanced itself every 8-15
            seconds while chat announced each step; the announcements arrived batched, the
            owner could not identify which level was visible, and that run was ruled
            INVALID as a selection oracle. Nothing here advances on its own.

            FOUR ANSWERS, NOT THREE. C1-C4 are judged TWICE and independently: once as the
            ACTIVE level for Printing, setup and actionable states, once as the QUIET level
            for a resting Ready and for a Completed or Unavailable acknowledgement. They
            may coincide; the harness does not assume it.

            WHY IT DEPENDS ON THE DIAGNOSTIC SCENE. Brightness has to be judged against a
            composition, and no Printer Unavailable composition is accepted — the one that
            reached the owner was rejected twice. The dependency makes an image that walks
            the backlight over a rejected product frame unbuildable: the diagnostic scene
            owns the screen, controller.cpp is not compiled, and the harness draws its own
            neutral instrument surface.

            NO PWM VALUE IS HARD-CODED. Each step names a ceiling and a fraction of that
            ceiling's LIGHT; the control percentage comes from
            display::percent_for_light_fraction(), the same function the product uses.

            MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH PRD_VALIDATION_SCENARIOS AND
            PRD_VALIDATION_OPERATIONAL_WALK: all three consume the same short press.

            This is instrumentation, not a product-screen proposal, and not product
            behaviour. Enable only in the hardware-validation profile.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTING_TIMELINE
        bool "Replay a print from JSON status bodies through the whole production path"
        default n
        help
            Drives the product from the PrusaLink boundary with no printer: a versioned,
            synthetic timeline of GET /api/v1/status response bodies goes through the
            PRODUCTION decoder, normalizer, events, store, reducer, projection and UI.

            WHAT IT IS FOR. Printing telemetry — the percentage, the remaining time, the
            elapsed time — could not reach the panel at all until this checkpoint, because
            the decoder read no job fields. The composition, the transitions between Ready,
            Preparing, Printing, Paused, Finished and back, the temperature treatment and
            the Active brightness all have to be LOOKED AT, and a real print takes an hour
            of a printer nobody wants to tie up. This compresses the print and leaves every
            stage downstream of the response body untouched.

            WHAT IT IS NOT. Not a PrusaLink server: no socket is opened, and the network
            stack, the worker task and the poll pacing are all bypassed, so it demonstrates
            nothing about the transport. Not a second UI: the panel shows the product's own
            composition. And NOT PHYSICAL ACCEPTANCE — the bodies are shaped to the
            Prusa-Link-Web OpenAPI contract and to Prusa-Firmware-Buddy v6.8.1's status
            renderer, and they are NOT captured from a printer. Field-set compatibility
            still rests on a future real capture, redacted into a firmware-specific fixture.

            The timeline covers Ready; Preparing with changing temperatures; Printing with
            several progress and time updates; Paused; resumed Printing; Finished; the job
            object being retired; and back to Ready. Two steps carry individually unusable
            optional fields, one omits time_remaining as v6.8.1 does when it has no valid
            estimate, and one carries additive members at three levels.

            It runs on its own, holding each state for 18 seconds — about 5.1 minutes for the
            run — because the question is about a sequence rather than a frame, and because a
            reviewer needs long enough to actually read a percentage, a remaining time, two
            temperature pairs and a brightness level. Each hold stays under the 20-second Quiet
            recession boundary, so the panel keeps the Active level being judged.

            Every step emits ONE serial row, led by `step N/17`, carrying the state's label and
            every projected value. The BOOT button stops it: short press single-steps regardless
            of the schedule, long press pauses and resumes, very long press restarts with a
            fresh generation. None of the three has a product meaning while this is compiled in.

            MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH THE THREE FLOWS ABOVE. The scenario sweep and the
            operational walk dispatch into the same store, and the brightness harness takes
            the backlight this flow needs the product display policy to own.

            This is diagnostics, not product behaviour. Enable only in the
            hardware-validation profile; the debug and release profiles must leave it off so
            no fixture data reaches a product image.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_ACCESS_REVIEW
        bool "Replace the print timeline with the access-screen review"
        default n
        depends on PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTING_TIMELINE
        depends on !PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTING_LOSS_REVIEW
        depends on !PRD_VALIDATION_SCENARIOS && !PRD_VALIDATION_OPERATIONAL_WALK
        depends on !PRD_UI_DIAGNOSTIC_SCENE
        help
            Reuses the existing synthetic-source lease and gesture isolation but replaces
            the print timeline entirely. Ten automatic 18-second holds exercise Connecting,
            authentication and unsupported failures, corrections, failed retries and Ready.
            The final Ready source refreshes every five seconds without changing sleep policy.
            No socket, credential, storage or renderer operation belongs to this producer.
            BOOT gestures are consumed and ignored; reconnect to restart only when instructed.
            Compile only for a dedicated diagnostic review image, never a product image.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTER_ERROR_REVIEW
        bool "Use printer-condition error sequence instead of access errors"
        default n
        depends on PRD_VALIDATION_ACCESS_REVIEW
        help
            Isolated synthetic observations through the normal reducer and UI. No network
            or credential writes. Includes first Error, Printing and Paused recovery,
            one 38-second stale interval, then stable Ready with normal sleep.

    config PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTING_LOSS_REVIEW
        bool "Continue the Printing timeline with real-time loss/recovery chapters"
        default n
        depends on PRD_VALIDATION_PRINTING_TIMELINE
        help
            After the 17 print steps, run four synthetic observation-loss chapters:
            Ready settled recovery, Printing entry recovery to Completed, Printing
            recovery during retirement fade, and Ready retirement/Off recovery.
            The model's real freshness and display timers are unchanged. Each loss
            waits for actual expiry; no future timestamp or shortened policy is used.
            Consequently the existing Degraded appearance is exercised too, not hidden.
            That appearance is an open UX finding, not accepted by enabling this run.

            After the chapters, refresh final Ready every five seconds indefinitely,
            without restarting display recession. Observe the post-run Quiet/Dimmed/Off
            cycle separately. Logs distinguish completion from manual skips or controls.
            Short press skips a chapter, long press pauses/resumes, very long restarts
            the entire print. All gestures remain diagnostic-only.

            Diagnostic builds only; absent from every non-selected target build.
            This adds no renderer, network request, reset operation or storage access.

endmenu
